```java
interface DiscountStrategy { double apply(double price); }
class NoDiscount implements DiscountStrategy { public double apply(double p) { return p; } }
class TenPercentOff implements DiscountStrategy { public double apply(double p) { return p * 0.9; } }

interface DiscountFactory { DiscountStrategy create(String tier); }
class DefaultDiscountFactory implements DiscountFactory { /* picks NoDiscount or TenPercentOff */ }

class DiscountLogger implements DiscountStrategy { /* wraps another strategy, logs the result */ }
```

Five types exist here to express what's really one idea — "compute a discount percentage by tier" — split across a Strategy pair, a Factory, and a Decorator that could just be a log statement.

```java
class DiscountCalculator {
    private static final Map<String, Double> RATES = Map.of("NONE", 1.0, "TIER1", 0.9);
    double apply(String tier, double price) {
        double rate = RATES.getOrDefault(tier, 1.0);
        double result = price * rate;
        log.debug("discount tier={} price={} result={}", tier, price, result);
        return result;
    }
}
```

One class replaces five, the logging is an inline statement instead of a wrapper class, and a new tier is one map entry — the actual variation (a tier-to-rate lookup) was never complex enough to need three separate patterns.
